Steps to reproduce the behavior:

Download 1.0 release or latest nightly Windows LibTorch binaries from pytorch.org
Extract files.
Check libtorch/include/ATen/cuda/CUDAConfig.h (1.0 stable) or
libtorch/lib/include/ATen/cuda/CUDAConfig.h (latest nightly)
Observe #define AT_CUDNN_ENABLED() 0
Compile the following test program, it will print "NO CUDNN?"

#include <iostream>
#include <torch/torch.h>

int main() {
    if (!torch::cuda::cudnn_is_available()) {
        std::cerr << "NO CUDNN?" << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
